{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2889 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "27be023bc32c4266717f4451d4d11dae236173e7c13d3ecce2e9f05cfe7be9cd" :edb/db-before-t 2888 :edb/db-after-t 2889 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536201 50 #inst "2026-10-09T20:08:11.807Z" 13194139536201 true] [17592186152092 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388652 13194139536201 true] [17592186152092 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388659 13194139536201 true] [17592186152092 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388666 13194139536201 true] [17592186152117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388631 13194139536201 true] [17592186152117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388673 13194139536201 true] [17592186152117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388680 13194139536201 true] [17592186152118 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388638 13194139536201 true] [17592186152118 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388645 13194139536201 true] [17592186152118 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388687 13194139536201 true] [17592186152559 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536201 true] [17592186152559 1100 "Given that $y_{1}(t) = t$ and $y_{2}(t) = t^{2}$ are solutions to the equation $t^{2}y^{″} - 2ty^{′} + 2y = 0$ for $t > 0$, what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" 13194139536201 false] [17592186152559 1100 "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)=t$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}$ are solutions to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-2t{y}^{′}+2y=0$ for $t>0,$ what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" 13194139536201 true] [17592186152559 1145 "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)=t$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{t-{t}^{2}}{2}\n$$" 13194139536201 true] [17592186152730 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536201 true] [17592186152730 1100 "Given that $y_{1}(x) = \\sin x$ and $y_{2}(x) = \\cos x$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n1. $y(x) = (\\sin x\\cos x)^{2}$\n2. $y(x) = x\\sin x\\cos x$\n3. $y(x) = \\sin x + \\cos x$" 13194139536201 false] [17592186152730 1100 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}$\n- $y(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" 13194139536201 true] [17592186152730 1145 "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a function and the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139536201 true] [17592186152732 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536201 true] [17592186152732 1100 "Given that $y_{1}(x) = \\sin (2x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n1. $y(x) = \\sin^{2} (2x)$\n2. $y(x) =-\\sin (2x)$\n3. $y(x) = x^{2}\\sin (2x)$" 13194139536201 false] [17592186152732 1100 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)$\n- $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" 13194139536201 true] [17592186152732 1145 "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388631 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388632 13194139536201 true] [17592186388631 1089 "q-131013" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388631 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536201 true] [17592186388631 1100 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{4x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-12y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-3x}}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{4x}+{e}^{-3x}$\n- $y(x)={e}^{x}$" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388631 1113 #uuid "a02f4b33-a5b8-5f4c-9305-d8176d9840e3" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388631 1145 "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-3x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{4x}+{e}^{-3x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{x}={e}^{4x}\\cdot {e}^{-3x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388633 13194139536201 true] [17592186388632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388634 13194139536201 true] [17592186388632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388635 13194139536201 true] [17592186388632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388636 13194139536201 true] [17592186388632 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388637 13194139536201 true] [17592186388632 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536201 true] [17592186388632 1041 "selection" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388632 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388637 13194139536201 true] [17592186388632 1110 #uuid "7bee083f-fb40-5275-a3fc-8147377d1d9d" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388633 1034 "I and III only" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388633 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536201 true] [17592186388633 1111 #uuid "97a4c0b0-f3de-5b28-981e-2234e33e316b" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388634 1034 "I only" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388634 1103 #edb/ref 1