**not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388640 13194139536201 true] [17592186388639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388641 13194139536201 true] [17592186388639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388642 13194139536201 true] [17592186388639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388643 13194139536201 true] [17592186388639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388644 13194139536201 true] [17592186388639 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536201 true] [17592186388639 1041 "selection" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388639 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388643 13194139536201 true] [17592186388639 1110 #uuid "571a1259-2474-51be-819c-5bacde8bc540" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388640 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388640 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536201 true] [17592186388640 1111 #uuid "4c73d9b4-d20a-5dc8-a807-39dd4ca925fa" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388641 1034 "II and III only" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388641 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536201 true] [17592186388641 1111 #uuid "5ba70b58-63f8-529c-971d-f86a0e16f589" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388642 1034 "I only" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388642 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536201 true] [17592186388642 1111 #uuid "f0fdd4c9-acbe-5997-8ccc-436971a7c379" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388643 1034 "I and II only" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388643 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536201 true] [17592186388643 1111 #uuid "f2e2e5ea-2b58-5561-b741-7c24c98a9f9c" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388644 1034 "II only" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388644 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536201 true] [17592186388644 1111 #uuid "6413f2a0-6ea1-5c9a-8dc0-7f9c41840497" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388645 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388646 13194139536201 true] [17592186388645 1089 "q-131022" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388645 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536201 true] [17592186388645 1100 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{4}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-7x{y}^{′}+16y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=-2{x}^{4}$\n- $y(x)={x}^{4}\\sqrt{x}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{e}^{x}}$" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388645 1113 #uuid "2081eb5b-57d7-57e6-a013-e3e8bcd2b543" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388645 1145 "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-2{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{4}\\sqrt{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{e}^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotient of a known solution and a non-constant function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388647 13194139536201 true] [17592186388646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388648 13194139536201 true] [17592186388646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388649 13194139536201 true] [17592186388646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388650 13194139536201 true] [17592186388646 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388651 13194139536201 true] [17592186388646 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536201 true] [17592186388646 1041 "selection" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388646 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388649 13194139536201 true] [17592186388646 1110 #uuid "7911dc6f-02db-56ba-930a-f61f1029be93" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388647 1034 "II and III only" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388647 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536201 true] [17592186388647 1111 #uuid "0588687c-c693-584f-bc4c-3549d000d3d1" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388648 1034 "I and III only" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388648 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536201 true] [17592186388648 1111 #uuid "0756c3a4-35c8-5711-ad57-4579fc25c723" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388649 1034 "I only" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388649 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536201 true] [17592186388649 1111 #uuid "e4017307-a832-54e6-bc2c-dc51aa3c6d3c" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388650 1034 "I and II only" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388650 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536201 true] [17592186388650 1111 #uuid "418ec1c5-491b-5b2b-be73-945bb782b374" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388651 1034 "II only" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388651 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536201 true] [17592186388651 1111 #uuid "c778259f-1272-5d91-8e86-459694de1c83" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388652 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388653 13194139536201 true] [17592186388652 1089 "q-131029" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388652 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536201 true] [17592186388652 1100 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{5}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-20y=0,$ what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388652 1113 #uuid "93ee6e63-1b10-5325-b502-afabadb6eb8e" 13194139536201 true] [17592186388652 1145 "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{5}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}.